Found it: `tryBase64Text` requires `compact.length >= 24` and decoded match `{16,}`. My test's userinfo "aes-256-gcm:pw" encodes to "YWVzLTI1Ni1nY206cHc=" — length 20 → below 24 → null → parseShadowsocks returns null.

That length floor (24) was designed for whole-subscription detection (avoid mistaking a short word for base64). But for ss userinfo it's too strict. Real ss links with short passwords would break. Root-cause fix: parseShadowsocks shouldn't use the "is this a whole subscription" heuristic. Add a dedicated strict base64 decode helper `decodeBase64Text` that doesn't impose the length floor, and use it in parseShadowsocks. Keep tryBase64Text (with floor) for whole-body detection.

Lazy fix: in subscription.js, add `function base64Loose(text)` used by parseShadowsocks:

```js
function decodeBase64(text) {
  const compact = String(text).replace(/\s+/g, "");
  if (compact.length === 0 || !/^[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}$/.test(compact)) return null;
  try {
    const decoded = Buffer.from(compact, "base64").toString("utf8");
    return /[\x20-\x7e\u4e00-\u9fa5]/.test(decoded) ? decoded : null;
  } catch { return null; }
}
```
And tryBase64Text = decodeBase64 with length>=24 requirement... but tryBase64Text is exported and used for whole body; keep it as is, and use decodeBase64 in parseShadowsocks.

